{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs36 Day 13 \u8212- A moment of admiration counts for more than you think\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Think of someone you truly look up to. A teacher, maybe. Or someone whose kindness you still remember.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a moment, you feel it. Real respect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's verses look at two feelings we can hold toward such a person. And what each one does to us.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Renewing the Bodhisattva Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i We say this short promise every day. Read it aloud if you can.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b Mind Training} {\i (recite 3 times)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 May all beings enjoy happiness and the causes of happiness. May all beings be free from suffering and the causes of suffering. May all beings never be separated from supreme happiness, free from suffering. May all beings rest in great equanimity, free from attachment and aversion to those near and far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b Refuge} {\i (recite 3 times)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 Until reaching the heart of buddhahood, I take refuge in the Buddha, I take refuge in the dharma, And in the sangha of bodhisattvas.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b Taking the Bodhisattva Vow} {\i (recite 3 times)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 Just as the buddhas of the past gave rise to bodhicitta And engaged step by step in the training of the bodhisattvas, So I too, for the benefit of all beings, will give rise to bodhicitta And engage step by step in that same training.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Today\u8217's Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Today\u8217's lines. First in Tibetan, then in simple English.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 \u3906?\u3908?\u3851?\u3934?\u3954?\u3906?\u3851?\u3921?\u3962?\u3851?\u3936?\u3921?\u4018?\u3936?\u3954?\u3851?\u3938?\u3986?\u4017?\u3939?\u3851?\u3942?\u4018?\u3942?\u3851?\u3942?\u4006?\u4017?\u3954?\u3923?\u3851?\u3926?\u3921?\u3906?\u3851?\u3939?\u3853? \u3853? \u3906?\u3939?\u3851?\u3919?\u3962?\u3851?\u3908?\u3923?\u3851?\u3942?\u3962?\u3928?\u3942?\u3851?\u3942?\u3984?\u4017?\u3962?\u3921?\u3851?\u3924?\u3938?\u3851?\u3926?\u4017?\u3962?\u3921?\u3851?\u3923?\u3851?\u3921?\u3962?\u3853? \u3853? \u3908?\u3923?\u3851?\u3942?\u3962?\u3928?\u3942?\u3851?\u3926?\u3942?\u3984?\u4017?\u3962?\u3921?\u3851?\u3924?\u3936?\u3954?\u3851?\u3906?\u4018?\u3908?\u3942?\u3851?\u3926?\u3934?\u3954?\u3923?\u3851?\u3926?\u3942?\u3984?\u3939?\u3851?\u3924?\u3938?\u3851?\u3923?\u3954?\u3853? \u3853? \u3921?\u3928?\u4017?\u3939?\u3851?\u3926?\u3938?\u3851?\u3906?\u3923?\u3942?\u3851?\u3924?\u3938?\u3851?\u3936?\u3906?\u4017?\u3956?\u3938?\u3851?\u3934?\u3962?\u3942?\u3851?\u3920?\u3956?\u3926?\u3851?\u3924?\u3942?\u3851?\u3906?\u3942?\u3956?\u3908?\u3942?\u3853? \u3853?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 If anyone harbors a negative mind toward such a child of the Buddha, they will dwell in hell for eons. The Sage said they will remain there for as many eons as there were moments of that negative mind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 \u3936?\u3964?\u3923?\u3851?\u3919?\u3962?\u3851?\u3906?\u3908?\u3851?\u3934?\u3954?\u3906?\u3851?\u3937?\u3954?\u3921?\u3851?\u3938?\u3926?\u3851?\u3921?\u3908?\u3851?\u3926?\u4017?\u3962?\u3921?\u3851?\u3923?\u3853? \u3853? \u3921?\u3962?\u3851?\u3937?\u3954?\u3851?\u3936?\u3926?\u4018?\u3942?\u3851?\u3926?\u3956?\u3851?\u3921?\u3962?\u3851?\u3926?\u3942?\u3851?\u3939?\u4023?\u3906?\u3851?\u3924?\u3938?\u3851?\u3936?\u3925?\u3962?\u3939?\u3853? \u3853? \u3938?\u3986?\u4017?\u3939?\u3851?\u3942?\u4018?\u3942?\u3851?\u3938?\u4003?\u3928?\u3942?\u3851?\u3939?\u3851?\u3921?\u3964?\u3851?\u3906?\u3939?\u3851?\u3910?\u3962?\u3923?\u3851?\u3924?\u3964?\u3942?\u3851?\u3904?\u4017?\u3908?\u3851?\u3853? \u3853? \u3942?\u4001?\u3954?\u3906?\u3851?\u3924?\u3851?\u3928?\u3954?\u3851?\u3936?\u3926?\u4017?\u3956?\u3908?\u3851?\u3921?\u3906?\u3962?\u3851?\u3926?\u3851?\u3908?\u3908?\u3851?\u3906?\u3954?\u3942?\u3851?\u3936?\u3925?\u3962?\u3939?\u3853? \u3853?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 However, if one generates a faithful mind toward them, its fruit will increase even further. Even when great harm befalls these children of the Buddha, no negativity arises, and their virtue naturally increases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 From the Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i A note from the teachers who explained this text.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's verses set two thoughts side by side. A hateful thought toward a good person, and a warm one. You might expect them to weigh the same. They do not. The warm one weighs far more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Ngulchu Thokme} makes this real with a picture. Imagine someone gives sight back to every blind person in the world. A vast kindness. Now imagine someone else simply looks at a kind, good-hearted person with real warmth. That quiet look, he says, brings even more good than giving back all that sight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the good you feel does not need to be grand. {\b Sazang Mati Panchen} adds that one moment of true respect outweighs a moment of anger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hold this today: {\b a warm thought is never small.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Aspiration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i A wish to close with.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 May the precious bodhicitta Arise in those who don\u8217't have it yet; And in those who already have it, May it not decline, but grow ever further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 By the merit of practicing The Bodhicharyavatara today May all living beings come to engage In the conduct of the bodhisattvas.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Today\u8217's Practice Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Your task today:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bring to mind one person whose goodness you respect. A teacher, a friend, anyone kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a few seconds, {\b let yourself truly admire them.} Feel the warmth, and do not rush past it.\par}
}
